2026-08-29  stevensko <skovraneksteven@gmail.com>

	* Fork of vcsh, rebranded as "conFig Files Manager"
	* Command renamed vcsh -> fig
	* Environment variables renamed VCSH_* -> FIG_*
	* Nothing is created under $HOME any more. Everything lives in
	  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/:
	    - one <repo>.fig/ directory per repo, containing:
	        <repo>.git/     the git directory
	        .figrc       per-repo shell rc (sourced), was config.d/<repo>
	        .gitignore      per-repo ignore file  (was ~/.gitignore.d/<repo>)
	        .gitattributes  per-repo attributes   (was ~/.gitattributes.d/<repo>)
	    - shared: .figrc (shell rc), .gitignore / .gitattributes /
	      .gitconfig, the .repos list, and the hooks/ overlays/ dirs
	  upgrade() points core.excludesfile / core.attributesfile at the
	  per-repo file if present, else the shared one. These files are no
	  longer tracked by the repo (they are outside $HOME).
	* The shared .gitignore is seeded with `*` on first run, so `git add .`
	  in a fig repo is safe by default; `fig <repo> add -f <path>`
	  tracks a new file. Trade-off: `fig list-untracked` returns nothing
	  unless you make a curated per-repo .gitignore (fig write-gitignore).
	* Hook/overlay dirs lost the -enabled suffix: hooks/ and overlays/.
	* Shell rc file renamed: ~/.config/fig/config -> ~/.config/fig/.figrc
	  (and per-repo <repo>.fig/config -> <repo>.fig/.figrc); system
	  file /etc/vcsh/config -> /etc/fig/figrc
	* Source script renamed vcsh.in -> fig.in; man page vcsh.1 -> fig.1
	* $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/.repos ("<name> <url> <branch> @tags," per repo)
	  is populated manually with `fig add <repo> [<tags>]` / `fig add --all`;
	  fig never writes it on its own (delete/rename leave stale lines).
	  There is no `manifest` command. `fig bootstrap` -- equivalently
	  `fig clone --all` / `-a` -- clones everything listed there that is
	  not already present. Built-in, no myrepos/gita.
	* .repos rows take an optional tag field after the branch, always stored
	  with a leading "@" and a trailing "," (`fig add <repo> a,b` writes
	  "@a,b,"; a leading "@" or trailing "," you pass is absorbed).
	  An untagged row is always cloned; a tagged row is opt-in and is cloned
	  only when `fig bootstrap --include=` names one of its tags, so a bare
	  `fig bootstrap` clones the untagged rows only. `--exclude=` always
	  wins; matching is case-insensitive, and a leading "@" on tags passed
	  to --include/--exclude is optional (matching `fig add`). A bare
	  "@tag" / "@a,b" positional is shorthand for --include; it works for
	  `fig bootstrap` and `fig clone` alike (`fig clone @laptop`), and
	  repeated include/exclude/@tag args accumulate. A missing trailing
	  newline on the last row is tolerated.
	* New: `fig config <git config args>` edits a shared git config file
	  ($FIG_GITCONFIG) that every repo includes via include.path;
	  set credential.helper, user.email, etc. once for all repos
	* `fig <repo> add <path>` now supplies `-f` automatically when the only
	  thing ignoring the named paths is fig's catch-all `*`. A path matched
	  by a real rule (a per-repo .gitignore line, .git/info/exclude, ...) is
	  NOT force-added -- git's "use -f" refusal still guards a deliberate
	  ignore. Skipped for bulk forms (. -A -u -n --dry-run ...), an explicit
	  -f, and $FIG_ADD_FORCE=no. Uses `git check-ignore -v` to classify.
	* commit/pull/push/foreach: the "<repo>: " label is now skipped entirely
	  when there is only one repo to act on -- with nothing to disambiguate,
	  it was just a line with nothing useful after it. Still shown once
	  there's more than one repo, but no longer separated by a blank line
	  between repos (removed per user preference).
	* New: `fig sync [<message>...]` -- add -u, commit, and push every repo
	  in one step (message defaults to "update"). Prints Adding.../
	  Committing.../Pushing... phase labels, but only with more than one
	  repo, same rule as the per-repo label above.
	* upgrade now sets status.showUntrackedFiles (default no, override with
	  $FIG_SHOW_UNTRACKED) so `git status` in a repo ignores $HOME
	* delete: use `git ls-files -z | xargs -0` so paths with whitespace,
	  glob characters, or non-ASCII bytes are removed correctly
	* Drop the myrepos (post-init-setup-mr) sample hook; bootstrapping is
	  now built in (see the .repos / bootstrap notes above)
	* Add install-local.sh for a no-autotools user-space install

